  "cut_point": "We are given the set $ M = \\{1, 2, 3, \\ldots, 2020\\} $, and we are asked to find the **smallest positive integer $ k $** such that **any subset $ A \\subseteq M $** of size $ k $ contains **three distinct numbers $ a, b, c $** such that:\n\n$$\na + b, \\quad b + c, \\quad c + a \\quad \\text{are all in } A.\n$$",
  "reflection": {
    "verified_state": [
      "The universe is $M=\\{1,2,\\ldots,2020\\}$.",
      "The problem asks for the smallest $k$ such that every $k$-element subset $A\\subseteq M$ has the stated property."
    ],
    "error_step": "**any subset $ A \\subseteq M $** of size $ k $ contains **three distinct numbers $ a, b, c $** such that:\n\n$$\na + b, \\quad b + c, \\quad c + a \\quad \\text{are all in } A.\n$$",
    "error_reason": "This incorrectly requires the three numbers a,b,c to be elements of A. The original condition only requires a,b,c to be distinct elements of M; only their pairwise sums must lie in A.",
    "new_direction": "Restate the condition using distinct a,b,c from M, not necessarily from A, and redo the extremal construction accordingly."
  }
}
